name  = matmul
shape = dense double-precision multiply-accumulate, 512x512
args  = 512
cflags = -ffp-contract=off
claim = the floating-point axis, which nothing else here touches. crc32 guards
        the integer path; the only measurement of the FP path was one note that
        said "about the same as C" once, and this is that turned into something
        a machine re-runs.
        ---
        FLOATING POINT MAKES THE SAME-ANSWER CHECK HARD, and that shaped the
        row more than anything else. `a*b + c` may be contracted into one fused
        multiply-add, rounding ONCE instead of twice, and the bits differ. Both
        precautions here were verified to be load-bearing rather than assumed:
        clang at its arm64 default prints a checksum of ...693 where every
        unfused implementation prints ...737, and Go's gc does the same until
        the multiply is wrapped in the `float64()` conversion its spec names as
        the way to prevent fusion. So the C row carries `-ffp-contract=off`
        (above) and the Go row carries that conversion. Mere needs neither: it
        emits `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF` itself (v0.1.315), and the runner
        deliberately does NOT pass the flag to Mere's cc -- if it did, the flag
        would stand in for the pragma the day the pragma stopped being emitted.
        Inputs are non-dyadic (divided by 1000, not 1024) so the arithmetic
        actually rounds; values that happened to be exact would agree whether
        or not anything fused, and the check would pass for the wrong reason.
        The answer is printed as the two halves of the IEEE-754 pattern,
        because a formatted double hides the low bits, which is where a
        contraction difference lives.
        ---
        No Python or Ruby row. The inner loop reads B down a column, so this is
        as much a memory-access benchmark as an arithmetic one -- identical for
        every row, so the comparison is fair, but it means the number is "this
        loop as written" and not peak FLOPs. Nobody writes that loop in Python
        or Ruby; they write a numpy or BLAS binding, which would time Fortran.
        And at this size the hand-written version takes 20 seconds, which would
        make the suite something nobody runs.
        ---
        The expected result is a TIE with C, and the row exists to notice the
        day it stops being one.

# 6.3 MB of matrices (three 512x512 double arrays) plus the region's own
# rounding, at v0.1.321. Tight, because this program's allocation is three
# fixed-size buffers: anything that moves it is a change in how Vec[R, float]
# is laid out, which is exactly what this row should notice.
alloc_min = 6291456
alloc_max = 16777216

# MoonBit needs strconv for the size argument.
mbt_imports = moonbitlang/core/env moonbitlang/core/strconv
